1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,242
Your brain can't feel
pain, not even a scalpel.

2
00:00:02,322 --> 00:00:04,814
It's the organ that tells you
when everything else hurts.

3
00:00:04,894 --> 00:00:07,372
But the brain itself has
no pain sensors at all.

4
00:00:07,452 --> 00:00:11,488
Scientists call these sensors nociceptors,
and brain tissue doesn't have them.

5
00:00:11,568 --> 00:00:14,475
That's why some patients stay
awake during brain surgery.

6
00:00:14,555 --> 00:00:17,264
They're sedated while the skull
is opened, then woken up.

7
00:00:17,344 --> 00:00:20,805
Being awake helps surgeons map
the brain and protect healthy tissue.

8
00:00:20,885 --> 00:00:22,854
So wait, why do headaches hurt so much?

9
00:00:22,934 --> 00:00:25,499
The pain comes from around
the brain, not from the brain.

10
00:00:25,579 --> 00:00:28,339
Blood vessels, muscles,
nerves in your face and neck,

11
00:00:28,339 --> 00:00:30,111
and the brain's protective layers.

12
00:00:30,191 --> 00:00:33,549
Your brain feels nothing, yet it
feels everything. Follow for more.

